1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. c4 b6 4. g3 Bb7 5. Bg2 c6 6. O-O d5 7. Nc3 Nbd7 8. cxd5 cxd5 9. Bf4 a6 10. Rc1 Rc8 11. Qb3 Be7 12. Rfd1 O-O 13. a4 Nh5 14. Bd2 Bd6 15. Na2 Qe7 16. Bb4 Nhf6 17. Ne5 Bxb4 18. Nxd7 Nxd7 19. Nxb4 a5 20. Nd3 Ba6 21. e3 Rc4 22. Bf1 Rfc8 23. Rc3 Rxc3 24. bxc3 Bc4 25. Qb2 Qd6 26. Ne5 Nxe5 27. dxe5 Qb8 28. Bxc4 Rxc4 29. Rd4 Rc5 30. e4 dxe4 31. Qd2 Rc8 32. Rd7 Rf8 33. Qd4 b5 34. axb5 Qxb5 35. c4 Qb1+ 36. Kg2 Qd3 37. Qxd3 exd3 38. Rxd3 Rc8 39. Kf3 g5 40. Rc3 a4 41. Ke3 a3 42. Kd4 Ra8 43. Rc1 g4 44. Ra1 Kf8 45. Ra2 Ke7 46. Kc3 Ra5 47. Kb4 Rxe5 48. Kxa3 { This is the point that black considers multiple endgames but desides that the king - pawn endgame will let him win although this is tricky and demand precise play } 48... Ra5+ (48... Rh5 49. Kb4 Rxh2 50. c5 h5 (50... Rh1 { But too much counterplay for white } { [%cal Gh1c1,Gh1b1] }) 51. Kb5 h4 52. gxh4 g3 53. Ra7+ { This does not work because of the check the black paw on g3 is lost anyway }) 49. Kb3 Rxa2 50. Kxa2 { This is worrying some because of the king can use the past pawn on the queen side as a decoy to gather up the pawns on the king side. The pawn on g4 is strong because it prevents the white pawns on the h2,g3 and f2 squares from moving. } 50... Kd6 51. Kb3 Kc5 52. Kc3 { Keeping the white king from e4 } { [%cal Gf7f5,Ge6e4,Ge6e5] } 52... e5 53. Kd3 f5 54. Kc3 e4 (54... h5 55. Kb3 f4 56. Kc3 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gc3d3,Gd3e4] } 56... e4 57. gxf4 { This will not work for black }) 55. Kb3 h6 56. Kc3 h5 57. Kb3 f4 { The breakthrough } 58. gxf4 e3 59. fxe3 h4 60. f5 Kd6 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Rf5f6] } (60... g3 61. hxg3 hxg3 { [%cal Gf5f8] } 62. f6 Kd6 63. c5+ Ke6 64. c6 g2 65. c7 Kd7 66. f7) 61. Kb4 (61. f6 Ke6 62. c5 Kxf6 63. c6 Ke7 64. c7 Kd7) 61... Ke5 { 0-1 White resigns. } { [%csl Ge5] } (61... g3 62. hxg3 hxg3 63. c5+ Ke5 64. c6 g2 65. c7 g1=Q 66. c8=Q Qb1+ 67. Ka5 { This is mostly a drwa for black } { [%cal Ga5c7] }) 62. c5 Kxf5 63. Kb5 Ke6 64. Kb6 g3 (64... Kd7 65. Kb7 g3 66. c6+) 65. hxg3 h3 (65... 